<SeoArticle title="Anti-Slop SEO Checklist for GEO Content | Coreweaver Labs" description="A practical pre-publish checklist for reducing AI-slop: original analysis, clear authorship, external citations, internal links, standalone answers, schema, and image alt text." canonical={canonical} eyebrow="GEO-COMPASS · editorial standard" heading="Anti-Slop Standard." lede="Signal has origin. Slop has volume without a defensible reason to exist." section="GEO-COMPASS">
  <p>This checklist is designed for humans and agents working in the same repository. It is a release gate, not a style preference. A page can be short and still pass. A page can be long and still fail.</p>
  <h2>Before writing</h2>
  <ul><li>State the reader’s problem in one sentence.</li><li>Identify the primary entity and the intended query set.</li><li>Collect primary sources, original observations, and relevant independent references.</li><li>Choose one proprietary concept, original framework, or canonical definition that the page can make useful.</li></ul>
  <h2>Before publishing</h2>
  <ul><li>The title and meta description accurately summarize the visible page.</li><li>Mason Nguyen or the correct author is named in the visible byline and Person schema.</li><li>The page links to its pillar, at least two adjacent pages, and a relevant author/entity page.</li><li>External claims cite authoritative sources at the point of use.</li><li>FAQs contain standalone answers and are visible to the reader.</li><li>DefinedTerm schema is used only for an actual visible glossary definition.</li><li>Images have descriptive filenames, dimensions, informative alt text, and captions when needed.</li><li>No paragraph exists solely to repeat keywords or inflate word count.</li><li>Uncertainty, scope, dates, and limits are explicit.</li></ul>
  <h2>Google’s people-first test</h2>
  <p>Google asks whether content provides original information or analysis, demonstrates expertise, identifies who created it, explains how it was produced when relevant, and exists primarily to help people rather than manipulate rankings. [<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" rel="nofollow">Google Search Central</a>]</p>
  <p>Coreweaver adds one operational question: <strong>could a skeptical reader trace the most important claim to a source or a clearly labeled first-party observation?</strong></p>
